      <pubDate>Fri, 30 Oct 2026 11:20:26 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div id="storytext" class="storytext storylocation linkLocation"&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Good morning. You're reading the Up First newsletter. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/newsletter/news" target="_blank"&gt;Subscribe&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; here to get it delivered to your inbox, and &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/podcasts/510318/up-first/" target="_blank"&gt;listen&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; to the Up First podcast for all the news you need to start your day.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Today's top stories&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It is the final stretch before Election Day on Tuesday &lt;/strong&gt;and candidates are making final campaign stops and delivering closing arguments. President Trump has been campaigning for at-risk Republicans, but he has abruptly switched to virtual rallies.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-148150" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 5648;         --source-height: 3177;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5648x3177+0+588/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd5%2Fc1%2F2440499a403bbcc5ada19a93094b%2Fgettyimages-2299538076.jpg" class="img" alt="A person walks past a voting sign at the East Point Library on Tuesday, the first day of early voting, in Atlanta." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 A person walks past a voting sign at the East Point Library on Tuesday, the first day of early voting, in Atlanta.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Megan Varner/Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;Trump's tour to boost voter turnout &lt;/strong&gt;has focused largely on competitive Republican-leaning states, NPR's Saige Miller tells &lt;em&gt;Up First&lt;/em&gt;. He's hosting 19 telerallies alongside in-person events. The shift in strategy, plus polling data, suggests that Republicans may face challenges, at least in the House, Miller says. Trump knows he's a key motivator for his party, but his &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6023035:x" target="_blank"&gt;strategy could also backfire&lt;/a&gt; by energizing Democrats.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;Alaska's governor has ordered an investigation following a &lt;em&gt;New York Times &lt;/em&gt;report &lt;/strong&gt;on the state's toss-up U.S. Senate race. The article revealed that Democratic campaign operatives were attempting to recruit a candidate, whom Republicans claim could confuse voters and &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/29/nx-s1-6022069/alaskas-all-important-senate-race-is-roiled-by-report-on-alleged-spoiler-candidate" target="_blank"&gt;siphon support from GOP incumbent&lt;/a&gt; Dan Sullivan. The candidate also shares the name Dan Sullivan. Democrat Mary Peltola, a former congresswoman, aims to flip the seat. Alaska Public Media's Liz Ruskin spoke with election law experts, who found no applicable laws but noted that the conduct described &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6023035:x" target="_blank"&gt;crosses moral and ethical lines&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;The Department of Justice subpoenaed election workers in Los Angeles&lt;/strong&gt; amid rising &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/29/nx-s1-6022560/doj-subpoenas-california-election" target="_blank"&gt;tensions with California officials&lt;/a&gt; over the Trump administration's efforts to identify noncitizens on voter rolls.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️&lt;strong&gt; At the start of this election cycle, Democrats appeared unlikely&lt;/strong&gt; to gain Senate seats because many races were in red states. But the ongoing war in Iran has changed the landscape. Here are the &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/30/g-s1-149975/2026-midterms-senate-map" target="_blank"&gt;key Senate races to watch&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;NPR member stations are compiling essential info for voters&lt;/strong&gt;, from guides to local races. Discover &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/30/g-s1-149560/2026-vote-midterm-election-local-voter-guides" target="_blank"&gt;resources in your community here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️&lt;strong&gt; The NPR App has all of NPR's election coverage in one place&lt;/strong&gt; — from the biggest stories across the country, to the issues that matter close to home. Browse headlines, playlists, podcast episodes, videos and more, all on the &lt;a href="https://npr.app/stqe/election-2026" target="_blank"&gt;2026 Election tab&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth has dismissed Army Lt. Gen. Brett Sylvia&lt;/strong&gt;, who oversaw planning at the Pentagon. Sylvia was also part of the Joint Chiefs of Staff. His removal comes as the U.S. and Israel prepare for a new potential round of attacks against Iran. Although Sylvia held a senior position in strategy, planning and policy, he was not directly involved in day-to-day operations regarding the Iran conflict.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;The Pentagon is investigating a leak but has not provided details&lt;/strong&gt;, NPR's Greg Myre says. Hegseth has dismissed several generals and admirals, often without explanation. These firings have disrupted senior military operations, Myre says. The U.S. currently has two aircraft carriers in the Arabian Sea, with a third, the USS Theodore Roosevelt, en route. According to Myre, it is &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6023035:x" target="_blank"&gt;unusual for the U.S. to have&lt;/a&gt; multiple carriers in one region. The last time it stationed three in the Middle East was during the 2003 invasion of Iraq.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thousands of military veterans with VA-backed mortgages have faced higher interest rates &lt;/strong&gt;and monthly payments since last year, after the Trump administration shut down a key part of the VA's home loan program. Before it was shut down, the Veterans Affairs Servicing Purchase (VASP) program, created under the Biden administration, helped tens of thousands of veterans who had fallen behind on their payments by rolling missed payments into a new, low-interest mortgage. The mortgage industry and housing groups warned the administration not to kill the program without first creating a viable replacement. Without one, vets had to modify loans at higher interest rates. An NPR investigation found that &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/30/nx-s1-5995628/trump-va-home-loan-program-veterans-paying" target="_blank"&gt;more than 15,000 veterans experienced&lt;/a&gt; monthly payment increases of at least $150 after their loans were modified due to financial hardship. More than 700 veterans saw their payments rise by $1,000 or more.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Behind the story&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;by &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/146944972/rob-stein" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Rob Stein&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, NPR's science desk correspondent and senior editor&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-150244" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 2800;         --source-height: 1575;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2800x1575+0+213/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2Fcd%2F4a241f91409c8b5294b9a8946af0%2Fgettyimages-2204900491.png" class="img" alt="Two hands embrace a heart for donation" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;I've been doing stories about the organ transplantation system for decades. One of the most fundamental guiding ethical principles in organ donation has long been the "dead donor rule." That rule, stated simply, says: Organs should only be retrieved for transplants from a person who is dead, and death should never be accelerated to try to boost the chances their organs will be usable. To make sure that's the case, there's long been a strict firewall between the doctors and nurses trying to save patients in the hospital, and the organ retrieval teams standing by to try to procure their organs.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;But over the years, I've heard many stories from family members who nevertheless felt pressured to donate their loved one's organs. So when I got a tip that the Organ Procurement &amp; Transplantation Network, which runs the nation's organ system, was planning to relax that firewall, I instantly knew that was a big story.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;I rushed to confirm the documentation that the network's board of directors had indeed endorsed the change, which would go into effect Nov. 18. I then reached out to bioethicists and others for their reaction to this major change. The revision was part of a set of new policies designed to boost donations and improve patient safety. Those changes were spurred by disturbing revelations about mistakes, including patients literally being wheeled into the operating room to remove their organs when they were still alive. Some of the changes were praised as improving safety. But many bioethicists and even some transplant surgeons and patient and organ donation advocates say relaxing the firewall between care and organ retrieval teams is dangerous. The fear is it could lead to families and caregivers discontinuing treatment too soon, and deepening distrust in the organ donation system.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;I explored what these rule changes will mean for organ donors. &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/28/nx-s1-6019613/organ-donor-ethics-risk" target="_blank"&gt;You can read about it here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Weekend picks&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resnx-s1-img-6304837" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 3377;         --source-height: 1900;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3377x1900+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa5%2F47%2Fc7a17b6a478990406652100b5387%2F969d2647-70de-4a5d-ac7c-d1cf7e2147bf.jpg" class="img" alt="Tom Rhys Harries in Clayface." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Tom Rhys Harries in Clayface.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Parisa Taghizadeh/Warner Bros. Pictures                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Check out what &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/sections/culture/" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;NPR&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;strong&gt; is watching, reading and listening to this weekend:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;🍿 Movies:&lt;/strong&gt; It's officially Halloweekend a.k.a. the perfect time to watch a horror movie. &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/26/nx-s1-6013644/clayface-puts-a-glaze-on-body-horror" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Clayface&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; &lt;/em&gt;embraces body horror to tell the origin story of the eponymous Batman villain. Tom Rhys Harries stars as an actor who turns to AI and nanotechnology for a radical solution after a mob boss disfigures him.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;📺 TV:&lt;/strong&gt; HBO's &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/30/nx-s1-6019428/war-is-a-little-trashy-and-very-delicious" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;War&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; features Dominic West and Sienna Miller as a British couple battling it out in a high-stakes divorce. Their fierce legal teams intensify the drama.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;📚 Books:&lt;/strong&gt; Elizabeth McCracken's &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/26/nx-s1-6017155/seven-stories-about-tammy-elizabeth-mccracken-review" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Seven Stories About Tammy&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt; weaves together interconnected short stories about a family's experiences with the eldest son's girlfriend.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;🎵 Music:&lt;/strong&gt; Slovakian singer Adéla has spent her life preparing for pop stardom. She has recently gained attention with her hit song "Nicole Kidman." In this interview with NPR's &lt;em&gt;World Cafe&lt;/em&gt;, she discusses the music industry and her debut album, &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/27/nx-s1-6011106/adela-album-prima" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;PRIMA&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;❓ Quiz:&lt;/strong&gt; This week brought pre-midterm drama. Plus, the rest of the world also held elections. Stay sharp — you'll &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i//2026/10/30/g-s1-150445/trump-midterms-ai-china-star-trek-dhs-news-quiz%20:x" target="_blank"&gt;nail at least three questions&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;3 things to know before you go&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="res1239166425" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4800;         --source-height: 2700;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4800x2700+0+45/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmedia.npr.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2F2024%2F03%2F18%2Fgettyimages-1031207830_custom.jpg" class="img" alt="Patrick Stewart was trained as a Shakespearean actor, and he brought that gravitas to all of his roles, along with a playful sense of purpose. He was knighted in 2010." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt;          &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Patrick Stewart was trained as a Shakespearean actor, and he brought that gravitas to all of his roles, along with a playful sense of purpose. He was knighted in 2010.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Jack Taylor/Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;      &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ol class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;Sir Patrick Stewart, who played Professor X in the X-Men franchise and the captain of the starship USS Enterprise on &lt;em&gt;Star Trek: The Next Generation&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/29/1230906945/patrick-stewart-dead" target="_blank"&gt;died on Wednesday at 86&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;A new study in&lt;em&gt; Science &lt;/em&gt;shows that a species of bird called the Manx shearwater &lt;a href="http://npr.org/2026/10/29/nx-s1-6019228/manx-shearwater-jet-lag-birds" target="_blank"&gt;experiences jet lag&lt;/a&gt; similar to humans. Researchers found that during long flights across the Atlantic, these birds become disoriented as their internal clock falls out of sync with the sun.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;In this week's installment of Far-Flung Postcards, NPR's Jane Arraf sends greetings from the former home of English mystery novelist Agatha Christie, located on the banks of the Tigris River in Baghdad. The house now &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/29/g-s1-150319/agatha-christie-baghdad-home" target="_blank"&gt;stands abandoned and crumbling&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This newsletter was edited by &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/859721331/suzanne-nuyen"target="_blank"   &gt;&lt;em&gt;Suzanne Nuyen&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
