      <description>&lt;div id="storytext" class="storytext storylocation linkLocation"&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Good morning. You're reading the Up First newsletter. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/newsletter/news" target="_blank"&gt;Subscribe&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; here to get it delivered to your inbox, and &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/podcasts/510318/up-first/" target="_blank"&gt;listen&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; to the Up First podcast for all the news you need to start your day.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Today's top stories&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Midterm ad spending has surpassed a record $11 billion&lt;/strong&gt;, according to Ad Impact. An NPR analysis found that candidates in four major Senate races &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/28/nx-s1-5940017/senate-campaign-fundraising-actblue-winred-ossoff" target="_blank"&gt;spent more than $15 million&lt;/a&gt; on fundraising ads in the 2025-26 election cycle. Meanwhile, President Trump has been campaigning in tight races across states like Texas, Mississippi, and Kansas.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-150274" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 3000;         --source-height: 1688;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3000x1688+0+156/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc9%2F7f0bc5a146378a9fb8b82095ae20%2Fgettyimages-2300238086-1.jpg" class="img" alt="Sen. Jon Ossoff, D-Ga., speaks during a campaign event on October 17, 2026 in Marietta, Georgia." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Sen. Jon Ossoff, D-Ga., speaks during a campaign event on October 17, 2026 in Marietta, Georgia.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Elijah Nouvelage/Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;Trump has not been sticking with his standard midterm pitch&lt;/strong&gt;, which includes touting GOP tax cuts and attacking Democrats, NPR's Danielle Kurtzleben tells &lt;em&gt;Up First&lt;/em&gt;. His jokes, including comments about Ohio Sen. Jon Husted's physique, entertain rally crowds but &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6020166:x" target="_blank"&gt;distract from the key issues&lt;/a&gt; the median voter cares about, like the economy and gas prices. Midterm turnout is usually low, but the GOP base still supports him, suggesting many MAGA votes are available, Kurtzleben says. But Trump needing to rally votes in very red states indicates a potential problem for his party.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The election in Israel is too close to call after voters turned out in record numbers&lt;/strong&gt; to decide whether to keep Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu in office or oust him for an alternative. Netanyahu was in a &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/28/nx-s1-6019423/israel-election-results" target="_blank"&gt;tight race with his top rival&lt;/a&gt; Gadi Eisenkot, a center-right former military chief of staff. Final vote tallies may take time, and if no bloc secures a majority, it could take days or weeks for party leaders to form a governing coalition and determine the next prime minister.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧&lt;strong&gt; Israelis went to bed thinking Eisenkot had the advantage&lt;/strong&gt;, but by morning, Netanyahu pulled ahead, making the election too close to call, NPR's Daniel Estrin says. Election officials tell NPR that they expect to &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6020166:x" target="_blank"&gt;finish counting votes by tomorrow&lt;/a&gt; afternoon. The leading candidate will have six weeks to form a governing coalition, likely competing for support from defectors in each other's camps. Eisenkot could gain the majority of lawmakers' support in parliament, thanks to a strong showing from Arab-led parties eager to see him as prime minister. Eisenkot's camp is hesitant to form a coalition with Arab parties due to mistrust and demonization of Arab political leaders after the Oct. 7, 2023, attacks, Estrin says. He adds that both candidates don't have a clear path to a majority coalition.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Federal Reserve typically combats inflation by raising interest rates. &lt;/strong&gt;While prices are currently rising faster than average wages, investors don't expect a rate hike at today's meeting.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;The Labor Department's latest cost-of-living report shows&lt;/strong&gt; consumer prices rose 3.7% in September compared to last year. NPR's Scott Horsley says that marks a larger annual increase than the previous month. Fed policymakers raised interest rates in September, but investors believe the central bank &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6020166:x" target="_blank"&gt;will remain low-key&lt;/a&gt; ahead of Election Day and may increase rates again in December.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Ebola outbreak has surpassed 10,000 people infected since its declaration in May&lt;/strong&gt;, according to the latest figures from the Democratic Republic of Congo. This outbreak has &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/27/g-s1-150136/ebola-outbreak-milestone-democratic-republic-congo-vaccine?renderPlatform=nprone_ios&amp;unified=true" target="_blank"&gt;spread faster than any in history&lt;/a&gt;. It is also the largest in geographic scope, spanning from Nigeria to Kenya, and is second in death toll with nearly 5,000 deaths. Around one-third of those who have died have been children.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;On the ground&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-146613" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 1120;         --source-height: 630;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1120x630+40+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fad%2F2e%2F9a236a454afaa7ae8442baf9d90d%2Funtitled-design-1-1.png" class="img" alt="Morning Edition 2026 Elections" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The results of the 2026 midterm elections will shape the final two years of President Trump's second term and serve as a precursor to the 2028 general election. This month, &lt;/em&gt;Morning Edition&lt;em&gt; is reporting from six states that could change the balance of power in Washington.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;With Election Day less than a week away, &lt;em&gt;Morning Edition&lt;/em&gt;'s Michel Martin went to the Midwest to hear from voters about what is important to them this midterm cycle. In Springfield, Ohio, the Trump administration has conducted an aggressive campaign to deport Haitian residents who have been living legally in the country with Temporary Protected Status. The administration revoked TPS for Haitians during Trump's second term. And in Michigan, Abdul El-Sayed has narrowly won the Democratic Senate nomination, a victory for the party's progressive wing. The swing state is important to both parties, which is why Trump and former President Barack Obama campaigned for their party's Senate candidate over the weekend.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Hear what voters in these two states had to say:&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 In Michigan, many &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6018541:x" target="_blank"&gt;voters expressed burnout&lt;/a&gt; as they worry about gas prices and healthcare.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 Trump's tariffs are hitting Michigan households harder than any other state because many people depend on cross-border trade. But voters affected said the &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6015161:x" target="_blank"&gt;issue isn't so clear-cut&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 Winning a statewide office in Michigan without Detroit is challenging, and El-Sayed didn't perform well there in the primary. Some Black Detroiters said they &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6016118:x" target="_blank"&gt;remain skeptical of him&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 Some Springfield, Ohio voters said they are voting early because they are angry about the &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-5908531:x" target="_blank"&gt;administration's treatment of Haitians&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Follow the reporting on &lt;/em&gt;Morning Edition&lt;em&gt;, NPR.org, in the NPR app and in this newsletter.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The NPR App has all of NPR's election coverage in one place — from the biggest stories across the country, to the issues that matter close to home. Browse headlines, playlists, podcast episodes, videos and more, all on the &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://npr.app/stqe/election-2026" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;2026 Election tab&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Life advice&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-149345" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 5587;         --source-height: 3143;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5587x3143+0+291/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd0%2Fa9%2Fabc100304af5a30f12badbb4791e%2Fgettyimages-1344024792.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5587x3143+0+291/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd0%2Fa9%2Fabc100304af5a30f12badbb4791e%2Fgettyimages-1344024792.jpg 600w, htt