adbb4791e%2Fgettyimages-1344024792.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5587x3143+0+291/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd0%2Fa9%2Fabc100304af5a30f12badbb4791e%2Fgettyimages-1344024792.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5587x3143+0+291/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd0%2Fa9%2Fabc100304af5a30f12badbb4791e%2Fgettyimages-1344024792.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5587x3143+0+291/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd0%2Fa9%2Fabc100304af5a30f12badbb4791e%2Fgettyimages-1344024792.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5587x3143+0+291/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd0%2Fa9%2Fabc100304af5a30f12badbb4791e%2Fgettyimages-1344024792.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5587x3143+0+291/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd0%2Fa9%2Fabc100304af5a30f12badbb4791e%2Fgettyimages-1344024792.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5587x3143+0+291/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd0%2Fa9%2Fabc100304af5a30f12badbb4791e%2Fgettyimages-1344024792.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5587x3143+0+291/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd0%2Fa9%2Fabc100304af5a30f12badbb4791e%2Fgettyimages-1344024792.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5587x3143+0+291/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fd0%2Fa9%2Fabc100304af5a30f12badbb4791e%2Fgettyimages-1344024792.jpg" class="img" alt="To free up more time in your life, writer Caroline Sylge proposes a creative idea: Give yourself a &amp;quot;say no&amp;quot; retreat. It&amp;#039;s a day to look at your calendar and thoughtfully decline obligations in the next few weeks and months." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 To free up more time in your life, writer Caroline Sylge proposes a creative idea: Give yourself a "say no" retreat. It's a day to look at your calendar and thoughtfully decline obligations in the next few weeks and months.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Juan Moyano/ Moment RF/ Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;As the holidays approach and appointments and commitments begin to pile up, author Caroline Sylge recommends a "say no" retreat — a day dedicated to figuring out which obligations you want to decline in the coming weeks and months. While it may seem unconventional to devote an entire day to saying no, this approach can help create space for what truly matters to you. Sylge shared with&lt;em&gt; Life Kit &lt;/em&gt;her &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/nx-s1-6011089/how-to-plan-a-say-no-retreat" target="_blank"&gt;suggested schedule for a "say no" retreat&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🙅 As soon as you wake up, list things you want to say no to. Identify the obvious tasks you want to avoid.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🙅 Take a break, then return to your retreat space and expand your "say no" list. Review requests, appointments, volunteer work and commitments for the coming days, weeks and months. Note the tasks you want to decline, and why.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🙅 After another break, review your list and start saying no. Start with a small win, like crossing off a demanding task. Next, focus on the tougher rejections that may involve saying no to someone you care about or work with.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;p&gt;For more guidance on a "say no" retreat, &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6008141:x" target="_blank"&gt;listen to this episode of NPR's &lt;em&gt;Life Kit&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. Subscribe to &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/newsletter/life-kit" target="_blank"&gt;the&lt;em&gt; Life Kit&lt;/em&gt; newsletter&lt;/a&gt; for expert advice on love, money, relationships and more.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;3 things to know before you go&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-150144" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4570;         --source-height: 2570;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4570x2570+69+120/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe6%2Ff5%2Fba78baac4439bbd5a6c84cb03334%2Facotar-9.JPG" class="img" alt="Dalia Luque, left, has her face painted by Amber Lovold, one of the organizers of a midnight release party at Politics and Prose in Washington, D.C. They are celebrating the publication of A Court of Splintered Harmony, the latest installment of Sarah J. Maas’ popular romantasy series." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Dalia Luque, left, has her face painted by Amber Lovold, one of the organizers of a midnight release party at Politics and Prose in Washington, D.C. They are celebrating the publication of &lt;em&gt;A Court of Splintered Harmony, &lt;/em&gt;the latest installment of Sarah J. Maas' popular romantasy series.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Ella Mitchell/NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ol class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;The latest installment of Sarah J. Maas' &lt;em&gt;A Court of Thorns and Roses&lt;/em&gt; came out on Tuesday, prompting thousands of fans to &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/27/nx-s1-6011109/acotar-a-court-of-splintered-harmony-sarah-j-maas" target="_blank"&gt;attend midnight release parties&lt;/a&gt; at bookstores nationwide.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;A federal judge has temporarily &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/27/nx-s1-6019192/east-potomac-golf-course-renovations-blocked-appeal" target="_blank"&gt;blocked the Trump administration's plans&lt;/a&gt; to further change East Potomac Park, the municipal D.C. golf course that the president wants to turn into a championship-level venue.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Harvard astrophysicists have found what they believe is a previously &lt;a href="https://www.wgbh.org/news/local/2026-10-27/potential-new-dwarf-planet-found-by-harvard-astrophysicists" target="_blank"&gt;undiscovered dwarf planet&lt;/a&gt; in our solar system. (via &lt;a href="https://www.wgbh.org/" target="_blank"&gt;WGBH&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This newsletter was edited by &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/859721331/suzanne-nuyen"target="_blank"   &gt;&lt;em&gt;Suzanne Nuyen&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
